a('prisoners-dilemma-two-person-iterated','repeated encounters allow contingent strategies to sustain cooperation','two players repeatedly choose cooperation or defection, score outcomes and respond using rules based on previous interaction','Strategy choices, noise and horizon assumptions strongly affect outcomes; a winning simulated strategy is not a universal prescription for social cooperation','reciprocity and repeated strategic interaction',('axelrodeff','computer tournaments compare contingent prisoner-dilemma strategies'),('axelrodcoop','reciprocity supplies a mechanism for the persistence of cooperation'),('nowak','other evolutionary mechanisms qualify explanations based solely on direct reciprocity'))
a('el-farol','independent boundedly rational forecasts can coordinate attendance near a capacity threshold','agents score attendance predictors against recent history and attend when their selected forecast predicts an uncrowded bar','Predictor design, memory and threshold choices shape results; convergence of mean attendance does not imply accurate shared beliefs or full rationality','inductive reasoning and adaptive crowding',('arthurbar','the original bar problem formalizes inductive expectation formation'),('fogel','evolutionary forecasting critically extends the original bounded-rationality model'),('minority','minority rewards isolate a related anti-crowding coordination mechanism'))
